Closes #403 when exact-head evidence is accepted.

## Scope

- refresh `docs/product/ARQ-UI-UX-TOKEN-LAYER-AUDIT.md` against current integration `main` @ `2d5a2ae73557049e0d5c2af01f6b0147942b1bcd`;
- add a narrowly scoped Vitest guard for shell/workspace layer drift;
- no broad restyle;
- no UX-1 overlay redesign;
- no `apps/web/src/App.tsx` mutation while #361 owns it.

## Exact-head movement handled

The audit initially inspected `14d333a225d6b606a8693f85246cacadb1c93ce3`. While the branch was being prepared, `main` advanced by one commit to `2d5a2ae73557049e0d5c2af01f6b0147942b1bcd`. That intervening commit changes only `docs/product/ARQ-UI-UX-WORKSPACE-OWNERSHIP-MAP.md`. No design-system, workspace implementation, shell token, modal/material, or `App.tsx` source changed. The branch was rebuilt directly on the new exact head.

## Exact-head corrections

- remove the stale prior finding that `tokens.css` still defines `--arq-z-modal: 400`; current `tokens.css` only imports the brand tokens;
- classify `WorkspaceSheet` raw layer 5, workspace menu raw layer 6 values, skip-link raw 20, legacy iPad layer 1 values, local refraction layer 1 and the `App.tsx` command-palette wrapper separately;
- record the command-palette transformed `zIndex: 10` wrapper as a #412 stacking-context risk without touching #361-owned `App.tsx`;
- record reduced-motion evidence and the refraction transition's non-canonical motion-token names for #486;
- route measured blur/shadow/compositing concerns to #402/#489.

## Guard boundary

The permanent test is intentionally narrow:

- pin the six canonical named global layer values in `shell-tokens.css`;
- scan only design-system `shell/**` and `workspace/**` for raw numeric `z-index`/`zIndex` declarations;
- reject raw values above 20 so ad hoc constants cannot enter the reserved named global layer range;
- permit the audited low local/debt range without freezing exact counts or locations;
- exclude renderer/geometry packages and `App.tsx`.

A raw value at or below 20 is not approval. The audit and #412 classifications remain authoritative.

## Evidence boundary

Focused `pnpm test` and the existing Chromium capability lane have already exercised the permanent guard and representative design-system dialog, workspace-layout, optical-glass and sheet-chrome paths on this #403 change set. The repository-wide `format:check` still reports seven unrelated pre-existing documentation files on `main`; the two #403 changed files are formatted to the repository's own Prettier contract. Because format precedes lint/typecheck/ZEUS drift in that job, those downstream steps are not claimed when baseline format debt stops the job.

The requested `node scripts/zeus.mjs compile --task ...` invocation cannot run in the connector-only execution environment and is recorded as blocked/not run. A synthetic all-overlays pointer/occlusion fixture remains #412 evidence rather than being invented here.

Closes the evidence gap for #396 without changing runtime UI.

What this does:
- re-anchors the workspace ownership map to `main` runtime head `14d333a225d6b606a8693f85246cacadb1c93ce3`;
- records the complete current open-PR ownership preflight and keeps PR #361 as the active `App.tsx` owner;
- links exact-head passing unit and real-Chromium capability evidence;
- maps desktop/tablet/phone, panel, viewport, overlay, no-project/project-open, read-only and recovery surfaces;
- corrects the stale claim that `WorkspaceSheet` restores previous focus;
- incorporates #403's current layer finding (`WorkspaceSheet` local `zIndex: 5`) without changing runtime styling;
- records exact safe UX-1 lanes and remaining serialisation around `App.tsx`.

No runtime code changes. No UX-1 implementation. No `App.tsx` mutation.

Closes the first implementation checkpoint of #494 when the required evidence is green.

## What changed

- added an additive editorial composition layer for the public site;
- added typed editorial primitives for large display fields, technical rails, critical state pairs and route transitions;
- recomposed PUB-001 around the existing governed product truth instead of adding new claims;
- kept `.arq opens` adjacent to `does not save back` as a first-class visual state pair;
- widened the public shell while leaving the existing static/reference baseline intact;
- copied `design/tokens/brand.v4.css` directly into the generated site and bridged marketing accent use to canonical brand tokens;
- removed the stale hard-coded `2026-08` revision month from the footer;
- preserved the no-client-JS and no-third-party-resource architecture.

## Authority and ownership

- integration authority resolved through #332;
- branch created from exact integration head `df04288c182bdfcee91f92e70b0e5d0017fc0628`;
- implementation branch: `codex/arq-public-site-editorial-v1`;
- owned slice recorded in #494.

## Deliberate limits

- no merge requested yet;
- no claim that the page is visually approved until screenshot/human review exists;
- no fake product screenshot was added. The hero uses an explicitly decorative abstract semantic-plan graphic;
- no PUB-002+ rollout in this PR yet;
- the separate anti-slop file mentioned by the owner has not been supplied, so no assumptions from it are encoded.

## Evidence needed

CI must establish build/type/test/language/claim invariants on this exact head. Visual and 200% zoom evidence still requires a browser-capable execution pass before this is ready to merge.

Related: #205, #332, #369, #377, #494

Closes #276.

## What changed
- add a typed schema-v1 -> v2 migration operation to the real ARQFS Worker;
- run migration only on the isolated OPFS working copy, never the selected source bytes;
- require writer ownership and a healthy, non-safe-mode candidate before migration;
- reopen/re-evaluate safe mode and run SQLite integrity after migration before adoption;
- wire real migration progress and failure states into `FileOpenPanel` instead of declaring migration verified immediately after staging;
- keep writer-locked or recovery-constrained older projects read-only rather than migrating them;
- make migration UI copy distinguish a real upgrade from an already-current working copy;
- extend the real Chromium/sqlite-wasm/OPFS capability proof with schema-v1 -> v2 migration and fresh-Worker persistence.

## Safety
- selected source bytes remain unchanged;
- a failed migration candidate is not adopted;
- future/unsupported/corrupt files retain the existing refusal or read-only behaviour;
- migration is gated by the Worker session's actual open verdict and safe-mode condition;
- post-migration open capabilities and SQLite integrity must both be healthy before the project can become writable.

## Evidence before PR
- focused migration, native-open policy, open-attempt guard and state-machine tests pass;
- repository-wide `pnpm typecheck` passes;
- `pnpm benchmark:e2e-arq-open` passes with the actual bundled ARQFS Worker, sqlite-wasm and OPFS, including schema-v1 migration on one Worker session followed by a fresh-Worker schema-v2 reopen;
- the proof run's accidental benchmark-results cleanup was repaired before this PR: the final diff contains no changes under `benchmarks/results`.

This PR is intentionally targeted at `main`, the newly proven stable integration ref. It does not rename the repository or change GitHub's default-branch setting.

Refs #332.

## Why
The repository still uses `claude/arq-cad-platform-research-ba8rav` as its canonical/default branch. Two production-relevant workflows are explicitly bound to that branch: full push CI and GitHub Pages deployment. Changing the default branch before those triggers are migration-safe would create a coverage gap.

## Change
- allow `CI` push runs on both the current canonical branch and `main`;
- allow `Deploy website` push runs on both the current canonical branch and `main`;
- update comments to make the temporary dual-trigger/rollback window explicit.

## Deliberately not changed
- repository default branch;
- repository name;
- any product/runtime code;
- PR/merge-group gates;
- Pages site path/origin logic;
- old canonical branch history.

`main` is only a candidate cutover ref here. This PR does not create it. After this lands, #332 can safely create the stable branch from an exact canonical SHA, change the default separately, verify CI/Engineering Gate/Pages, and retain the old branch for rollback before removing the temporary dual triggers.

## Rollback
Reverting this PR restores the previous single-branch triggers. No history rewrite or branch deletion is involved.

Completes the remaining ledger integration tracked by #321 after #349 and #348 landed.

## Outcome
Bind repository-intelligence evidence to ZEUS's existing evidence and gate ledgers without creating a second state system or weakening Engineering OS authority.

## Changes
- add a compact shared repository-ledger metadata contract recording the exact graph query, resolution, truncation, fingerprint, revision, branch, actual provenance and verification frontier;
- let `zeus evidence init` opt into graph-backed evidence with repeatable `--graph-seed` and tier;
- bind graph-backed evidence entries to the current graph fingerprint and refuse protected `verified` claims when graph state is stale, unresolved, ambiguous, uncertain/truncated, or uses provenance outside the hard-gate allow-list;
- make graph-backed evidence reports drop out of green when current graph binding no longer matches recorded verified claims;
- add a graph-aware `zeus gate` wrapper that reuses the existing gate ledger, monotonically raises risk/tier for protected or uncertain graph evidence and requires the graph verification frontier in addition to existing repository gates;
- preserve `scripts/zeus-gate-ledger.mjs` as the underlying state machine/store. No duplicate gate ledger is introduced;
- wire `pnpm zeus:gate` and `zeus gate` to the graph-aware wrapper;
- add focused regressions for protected escalation, required graph checks, stale/uncertain/truncated fail-closed behavior, non-hard-gate provenance rejection, evidence fingerprint binding and graph-backed gate start;
- refresh the governed `engineering/ops/generated/engineering-context-v5.json` after Engineering OS correctly detected that the new control-plane files changed repository context.

## Scope hygiene
Fresh branch from canonical `9a7e8216cd69549aa489d14b8c1a59ff57c0a87c`. Eight files differ: seven intended ledger/runtime/test surfaces plus the required generated Engineering OS context. No temporary validation/formatter/helper workflow, old graph ancestry, product code, persistence implementation, geometry implementation, release workflow or Engineering OS policy change.

## Validation before PR
A temporary push-only workflow proved pinned formatting, graph build, syntax checks and the focused ledger regression suite. The temporary workflow is not present in this branch. A separate helper workflow regenerated only the governed Engineering OS context and was then removed from its helper branch.

## Invariants
- graph evidence may raise requirements, never lower existing task/path/gate requirements;
- inferred/dynamic provenance cannot satisfy protected hard gates;
- stale, missing, ambiguous, uncertain or truncated graph coverage cannot certify protected evidence;
- current evidence/gate ledgers remain the only runtime state stores;
- Engineering OS remains merge/release authority.

## Parent issue
If exact-head ZEUS repository intelligence ledgers, Build, Security, full CI and Engineering Gate are green, #321's runtime + compile/impact + evidence/gate acceptance can be reassessed for closure.

Supersedes #344 and advances #321 from the current canonical integration head.

## Outcome

Connect the merged SHA-bound repository-intelligence runtime to ZEUS's canonical compile and impact paths without replacing the existing task/path classifiers or lowering any gate.

## Changes

- expose the graph runtime through `node scripts/zeus.mjs graph ...`;
- let `zeus compile` request bounded graph evidence with repeatable `--graph-seed <seed>` arguments;
- attach fresh/stale graph status, seed resolution, fingerprint, context/impact and verification frontier to compiled JSON;
- monotonically escalate compiled contracts when protected/uncertain graph evidence requires stronger risk, tier, review or checks;
- append a concise repository-intelligence section to human compile output when graph evidence is requested;
- feed changed-file seeds into `zeus impact` and translate graph verification commands into additive ZEUS checks;
- raise impact risk when graph verification is protected or uncertain;
- stale/missing graph state fails closed instead of being interpreted as safety;
- exclude mutable ZEUS runtime ledgers/caches from source-state invalidation so writing runtime evidence does not spuriously stale the graph;
- extend the repository-intelligence workflow with compile/impact integration regressions.

## Scope hygiene

Rebuilt byte-for-byte from #344's final intended file contents onto canonical SHA `d1cac742537015bc4cb18bcdbf8d36ea4556796b`. This branch is exactly one commit ahead, zero behind, with seven intended files changed. #344 had accumulated 17 incremental commits and was two commits behind canonical.

## Invariants

Graph evidence may raise risk/tier/check/review obligations but may never lower them or remove existing gates. Renderer/cache/adapter traversal does not gain semantic authority. Missing/stale graph coverage is explicit uncertainty. No external graph service, network lookup or second evidence ledger.

## Parent status

This covers #321's compile/impact integration. Keep #321 open after merge because #345 tracks the remaining evidence/gate-ledger integration.

## Merge condition

Merge only after ZEUS repository intelligence, Build, Security, full CI and Engineering Gate pass on the exact head.

Refs #326.

## Outcome
Upgrade the existing `pnpm zeus doctor` command from a raw environment dump into the one-command ZEUS operator status requested by #326. This reuses current ZEUS ledgers and repository intelligence rather than creating another state system.

## What it reports
- canonical base/current branch and ahead/behind drift;
- task, plan and delivery-state identity;
- mode, risk tier, blast radius and reversibility, with path impact allowed to raise but never lower the task classification;
- direct files, affected packages and languages;
- relevant sections of the canonical invariant register and explicit `.arq` persistence/recovery implications;
- repository-graph freshness and downstream impact, explicitly labelled `advisory-navigation-only`;
- evidence state, current/stale/failed gate evidence, required reviewers and Engineering OS 5.0 readiness;
- exactly one next action: `INSPECT`, `NARROW`, `IMPLEMENT`, `BENCHMARK`, `TEST`, `FIX`, `REVIEW`, `OPEN PR`, `WAIT`, or `RELEASE`.

## Safety / authority
- read-only status command, no auto-mutation, auto-risk-lowering, auto-verification, merge, release or external write;
- Engineering OS remains merge authority and overall status cannot be green when its applicable gate ledger is not green;
- graph output remains advisory and uncertainty/truncation is surfaced rather than treated as truth;
- canonical-base discovery reuses the existing changed-path/reviewer resolver and fails closed when the base cannot be established;
- human output carries no ANSI-only meaning; versioned `zeus-doctor/v1` JSON is available with `--json`; `--details` expands diagnostics.

## Regression coverage
- deterministic case for every allowed next action;
- green/partial/blocked/failed status cases;
- `.arq`, geometry and UI invariant routing;
- stable human snapshot with exactly one `Next:` line and no ANSI dependency;
- focused workflow runs syntax, status snapshots, ZEUS drift guard and ZEUS validation.

## Scope
Three files: the existing doctor implementation, its new regression suite and a focused workflow. No product code, package/lockfile, persistence implementation, geometry implementation, Engineering OS policy, release authority or repository settings changed.

Refs #321.

## Outcome

Connect the merged SHA-bound repository-intelligence runtime to ZEUS's canonical compile and impact paths without replacing the existing task/path classifiers or lowering any gate.

